    "When recent history shows a sharp upward spike followed by high volatility (e.g., Example 1-3), the model systematically underestimates the peak and fails to capture the subsequent mean-reversion; apply a downward correction of 5-8% to the forecasted trajectory.",
    "When sentiment is near-zero or low while price history exhibits extreme volatility, the model's linear extrapolation creates significant lag; introduce a non-linear adjustment factor that penalizes straight-line projections during high-variance regimes.",
    "In bearish or sideways consolidation phases (e.g., Example 4-5), the model tends to over-smooth downward trends and under-predicts sudden bounces; apply an upward correction of 3-6% when volatility spikes occur despite low sentiment scores.",
    "The model consistently ignores the 'mean-reversion' effect after extreme moves, assuming a constant drift; correct forecasts by adding a volatility-weighted decay term that pulls predictions toward the historical average rather than the linear trend."
  ]
